# Reference reverse-proxy / TLS terminator (T-00.7 / T-00.85, ops-envelope §9).
#
# Caddy terminates TLS and forwards to the NestJS app on :3000, stamping
# X-Forwarded-Proto so the app's IngestAuthGuard trusts the scheme (REQUIRE_TLS
# posture). Replace `analytics.example.com` with the real host; Caddy then
# auto-provisions + renews a cert.
#
# SANCTIONS / INTERNET-SHUTDOWN GUIDANCE (ops-envelope §9): during a shutdown
# Let's Encrypt HTTP-01 AND DNS-01 can both fail and certs expire → HTTPS goes
# dark. Mitigations:
#   - use DNS-01 via an EXTERNAL nameserver (a `tls { dns <provider> }` block with
#     an offshore DNS API), so validation does not need inbound reachability;
#   - obtain LONG-LIVED certs where the CA allows, or a commercial cert;
#   - monitor cert expiry and ALERT well before it (see docs/reverse-proxy.md) so
#     a stalled renewal is caught before the cert lapses.

analytics.example.com {
	encode gzip
	# Forward the client-facing scheme so the app's TLS-refusal guard passes.
	reverse_proxy app:3000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	}
}

# For a plain-IP / no-DNS dev box you can instead expose the app over Caddy's
# internal self-signed CA:
#
#   :443 {
#       tls internal
#       reverse_proxy app:3000 {
#           header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
#       }
#   }
